<Doc
  title="Pull requests"
  description="See, open, review and merge the pull request of the branch on screen, from the dashboard and the Chrome side panel."
  canonicalPath="/docs/pull-request/"
>
  <p>
    The Pull Request view of <a href="/docs/workspace/">Workspace</a> shows the pull request of the
    branch checked out in the selected workspace, or the form that opens one. It works for
    repositories on GitHub, through the account <a href="/docs/github/">connected in Settings</a>:
    what you see and do there is what that account can see and do on GitHub, with the permissions
    the app was given. A project whose remote is elsewhere is told so.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reading">What it shows</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The request.</strong> Number, title, state (open, draft, merged or closed), author, head and base, what changed, and whether GitHub considers it mergeable. When it does not, the reason GitHub gives.</li>
    <li><strong>Checks.</strong> Every check run and commit status on the head commit, failing ones first, then those still running, then the ones that passed, which fold away when there are more than a handful. Each opens on GitHub.</li>
    <li><strong>Conversation.</strong> Comments, review comments with their file and line, and reviews with their verdict, in order. Your own comments can be edited; a new one is posted from the box at the bottom. Markdown is rendered.</li>
    <li><strong>The diff.</strong> What the branch adds over the base, read from the machine that holds the workspace against the merge base, so it matches what you would push.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The view refreshes on its own every minute while it is on screen, and on demand.</p>

  <h2 id="opening">Opening one</h2>

  <p>
    With no pull request for the branch, the form asks for a title, which starts as the branch's
    name, the base branch, and a description. The branch is pushed first, with an upstream when it
    has none, so the request is made of what the machine has; then the request is opened, as a
    draft if you choose. With an <a href="/docs/ai-assist/">assistant linked</a>, a button writes
    the title and description from the branch's commits and diff.
  </p>

  <h2 id="acting">Acting on it</h2>

  <p>
    The button under the request names what comes next: mark a draft ready, or merge with the
    repository's preferred method. The chevron holds the rest: the other merge methods the
    repository allows, converting to a draft, editing the title or base, and closing. Merging and
    closing ask first. Nothing here deletes a branch; GitHub does that on merge when the repository
    is set to.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ship">Ship changes</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Ship changes</strong>, under the chevron of the commit button in Source Control, does
    the whole trip at once and needs an assistant and GitHub linked: it stages every change, has the
    assistant write the commit message, creates a branch off the base when the workspace is on it,
    commits, has the assistant write the pull request, pushes, and opens the request. Each step is
    shown as it runs, with the branch name and the titles the assistant chose. The first failure
    stops the chain where it is; nothing is undone, and the message says which step it was.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recorded">What is recorded</h2>

  <p>
    Opening, editing, merging, closing and commenting appear in the audit stream by name, with the
    project and workspace. Titles, descriptions and comments are never recorded.
  </p>
</Doc>
